# This table mainly conforms to the rules of "Hasegawa Rokuten Kanji",
# and partly to the Braille Authority of Japan's standard Japanese
# braille. Rokuten Kanji, or 6dot Kanji Braille, was devised by Sadao
# Hasegawa in 1972. Rokuten Kanji data is available at this site: 六点
# 漢字 日本語点字体系 (The system of Rokutenji Japanese Braille)
# <https://github.com/code4sabae/tenji/blob/master/README.md>. Sadao
# Hasegawa's essays on why and how he devised Rokuten Kanji is
# available at this site (in Japanese): 六点漢字の自叙伝
# (Autobiography of Rokuten Kanji)
# <http://www.wesranet.com/6ten/txt6idx.html>.
#
# Rokuten Kanji's purpose was to enable Japanese Braille users to be
# able to write Kanji characters with computers using 6dot Braille
# system. In Rokuten Kanji braille system, there are corresponding
# braille symbols for each kanji character. With Rokuten Kanji braille
# system, you can transcribe hiragana, katakana, Roman alphabet, and
# kanji directly. In Rokuten Kanji system, each Kanji character
# consists of 2 parts--one for "On Yomi (reading)", the other for "Kun
# Yomi". On Yomi is Chinese origin reading (or pronunciation) and Kun
# Yomi is Japanese native reading. For example, the Kanji character
# for a mountain "山" has On Yomi "san" and Kun Yomi "yama". Rokuten
# Kanji Braille symbol for the character mountain "山" is san-ya,
# conbination of "san" and "yama"'s "ya". Each Rokuten Kanji Braille
# symbol is 3 Braille cells, 2 cells for Kun Yomi, and 1 for On Yomi.
# And the first cell is also the Kanji indicator, which differentiate
# from Kana Braille. Japanese standard braille is the braille script
# of the Japanese language, but not necessarily to Japanese "written"
# language. Japanese written language consists of hiragana, katakana,
# Roman alphabet, and kanji. It transcribes Japanese more or less as
# it would be written in the hiragana or katakana syllabaries, without
# any provision for writing kanji. Japanese standard braille is
# maintained by the Braille Authority of Japan: http://braille.jp/.

